*** COMMAND LINE ***

formic <format_file> -i <input_file> [-D <defined>=<definer>] [-F <source_file_name>] [-r]

format_file         formic script
input_file          X++ source
defined/definer     defines variable 'defined' with value 'definer'
source_file_name    adds 'source_file_name' to list of files to select from


The 'format_file' is obligatory.  If there is no 'input_file' then Formic
takes input from stdin. Both -D and -F are optional and  may be repeated any
number of times.

-D <defined>=<definer> is equivalent to $(SET <defined> <definer>), q.v.

-F <source_file_name> adds file names to a selection list.  The 'infiles' predicate, q.v.,
uses this list to restrict selection of items.  This mechanism allows Formic to ignore
classes and functions that occur in preprocessed C++ source, but are irrelevant for
present purposes.

If there is no -F on the command line the default is to select from _all_ files
in the input.

-r changes the expected line separator character from '\n' to '\r'.

*** HEADER LINES ***

These occur at the beginning of a Formic script and their
effects are global. They are not output and their newlines
are dropped.


** Lexical Extensions **

$LEX xpp_type instance

xpp_type:
	PROTECTION_TYPE
	STORAGE_CLASS
	FUNCTION_SPEC
	TYPE_NAME
	CLASS_ATTRIBUTE
	PRE_INSTANCE_ATTRIBUTE
	POST_INSTANCE_ATTRIBUTE
	PRE_METHOD_ATTRIBUTE
	POST_METHOD_ATTRIBUTE

instance:
	(any alphanumeric string)

Before the X++ program is parsed 'instance' is added to the list
of tokens of type 'xpp_type'.


** Comment Substitutions **

$COMMENT_STUB "old_string" "new_string"

When class or function comments are output, 'new_string' is
substituted for 'old_string'.  This substitution is useful
when extracting comments from code to include in documentation
that is to be printed with a text-formatting tool.  The double
quotes around 'old_string' and 'new_string' are obligatory.
A literal double quote in a string can be escaped as '\"'. 
The C++ escape sequences '\n', '\t' and '\ddd' are expanded.


*** FORMATTING ***

** Comments **

$/   <text>          The remainder of the line is ignored by formic

$//  <text>          The remainder of the line and the followng \n is ignored

$/// <text>          The remainder of the line, the followng \n and all
                     following whitespace is ignored.


White space between the segments of ants is ignored.

If an ant occurs at the end of a line, the following newline
is dropped.  If the newline is desired in the output it can be
preserved by following the ant with $/.

Example:

	$	(FOR CLASSES)
	$		(CLASS)$/
	$		(FOR FUNCS)
		$		(FUNC)$/
	$		(ROF)
	$	(ROF)

run over

	class foo {
		int ff1();
		int ff2();
	};

	class bar {
		int bf1();
		int bf2();
	};

yields

	foo
		ff1
		ff2
	bar
		bf1
		bf2

The indentation pattern of left parens reflects the structure
of the Formic program, the indentation pattern of dollar signs,
that of the output.

The point here is that by judicious use of these formatting
devices it is possible to write a Formic script so that it can be
read as a program for generating derived text or as an abstract
example of the text to be generated, whichever seems appropriate.
In some cases (not all, unfortunately) it is possible to write
the Formic script so it can be read either way.



** Escape Sequences **


$$					escape sequence for '$'

$#					escape sequence for '#'


Formic scripts can be processed with cpp to take advantage
of its macro expansion and file inclusion features.
If this is done the $# sequence can be used to shield any '#'
that is intended to remain in the formic script.

Example:

	foo.f contains:

		this is foo

	bar.f contains:

		#include foo.f
		$#include foo.h
		etc ...

	'cpp bar.f > Bar.F' yields

		this is foo
		$#include foo.h
		etc ...

	formic Bar.F -i ... yields

		this is foo
		#include foo.h
		etc ...

BUG NOTE: '#' is treated specially by cpp only at the
beginning of a line, but it must be escaped in Formic
wherever it occurs (except inside an ant).
 

*** ANNOTATED SYNTAX ***

format:
	fcode(opt)

fcode:
	fcode(opt) item

item:
	for_clause
	if_clause
	switch_clause
	break_ant
	var_ant
	assig_ant
	sep_ant


** For Loops **

for_clause:
	for_ant fcode(opt) rof_ant

for_ant:
	$ ( FOR plural ordering(opt) criterion(opt) )

ordering:
	BY predicate_0

rof_ant:
	$ ( ROF text(opt) )

break_ant:
	$ ( BREAK text(opt) )

text:
	(any sequence of characters except $, parentheses, and 
	angle brackets) 
	
A FOR ant selects a list of items from the X++ source.
The type of item in the list is determined by the 'plural',
the selection of items by the 'criterion' and the sequence
by the 'ordering'.

The name of the iteration variable is that of the type 
associated with the 'plural', normally the corresponding
singular word in ordinary English.  This variable is referred
to in these notes as the "current" class, function, etc.

A break_ant causes an exit from the immediately enclosing loop.

The 'text' in BREAK, ROF and other cases mentioned below is
ignored by Formic.  It may be used optionally to comment
the ends of nested constructs.

Example:

	$(FOR CLASSES BY name base: Foo)
	$	(CLASS)$/
	$	(IF CLASS name: Bar)
	$		(BREAK)
	$	(FI)
	$(ROF CLASSES)

The 'for_ant' produces a list of classes in alphabetic order
by name, which are subclasses of the class Foo.  The 'for_clause' 
iterates over this list printing names until it prints the name
Bar, then exits.


** If Branches **

if_clause:
	if_ant fcode(opt)                     fi_ant
	if_ant fcode(opt) else_ant fcode(opt) fi_ant

if_ant:
	$ ( IF singular criterion(opt) )
	$ ( IF plural   criterion(opt) )

else_ant:
	$ ( ELSE text(opt) )

fi_ant:
	$ ( FI text(opt) )


The 'if_ant' produces a boolean value that controls branching. 

In 'singular' case  the value of the 'if_ant' is TRUE if the criterion
rpoduces a boolean that equals TRUE or if it produces a string that is non-null.
In 'plural' case the value is TRUE if the criterion produces a non-empty list.

Example:

	$(FOR CLASSES)
	$	(IF CLASS base)$///						singular - criterion has string value
			$(CLASS) has a superclass $//
	$	(FI)
	$	(IF CLASS base: Foo)$///				singular - criterion has boolean value
			which is Foo$//
	$	(FI)$/
	$	(IF FUNCS name: bar)$///				plural
			$(CLASS) has methods named bar
	$	(FI)
	$(ROF)


run over

	class Foo {
		int bar();
		void bar (int);
	};
	
	class Bar : public Foo {
	};
	
	class Baz : public Bar {
		int bar();
	};


yields

	Foo has methods named bar
	Bar has a superclass which is Foo
	Baz has a superclass 
	Baz has methods named bar


** Switch Branches **

switch_clause: 
	switch_ant case_list defl_case(opt) hctiws_ant

case_list:
	case_ant fcode(opt)
	case_list case_ant

case_ant:
	$ ( CASE criterion )

defl_case:
	defl_ant fcode(opt)

hctiws_ant:
	$ ( HCTIWS text(opt) )


A switch_clause is the exact semantic equivalent of a nested
sequence of if_clauses. 

Example:

	$(SWITCH ARG)
	$(CASE type: int)
		it's an int 
	$(CASE type: long)
		it's a long
	$(DEFAULT)
		it's not int or long
	$(HCTIWS)

is a more compact way of writing

	$(IF ARG type: int)
		it's an int
	$(ELSE)
	$	(IF ARG type: long)
		it's a long
	$	(ELSE)
		it's not int or long
	$	(FI)
	$(FI)

In particular, cases in Formic are mutually exclusive.  They do NOT
"fall through" as in 'C'. If more than one CASE matches the variable,
the first one will be taken.


** Criteria **

criterion:
	simple_criterion
	! criterion
	| criterion criterion
	& criterion criterion

! is negation, & is logical conjunction, | is logical disjunction

Example:

	$(IF FUNC & ptr ! | root: int root: char)

The 'criterion' should be read: "is both a pointer and neither to root
nor to char" i.e. the 'if_ant' selects functions which return pointers
to anything other than ints or chars.

simple_criterion:
	predicate_0
	predicate_1 rval
	predicate_2 rval rval
	predicate_3 rval rval rval

rval:
	string
	derived_string

derived_string:
	< singular simple_criterion(opt) >
	< plural   simple_criterion(opt) >

The code between the angle brackets is evaluated in the same way as
it would be in a var_ant, but the resulting text is returned as a
string to be used in the same way as a literal string. 

Example:

	$(FOR ARGS)
	$	(IF ARG type: <FUNC type>)
			*same*
	$	(FI)
	$(ROF)

This script would print the string '*same*' once for every parameter
of a function whose type is the same as its return type.
		

string:
	(any sequence of alphanumerics or any sequence of
	alphanumerics, spaces, and tabs surrounded by double quotes)

Formic recognizes the 'C' escape sequences \n, \t and \ddd

Example:

	$(SET foo "\003")
	$(foo)

This script outputs control-C.



** Formic Variables **

var_ant:
	$ ( singular simple_criterion(opt) modifier_list(opt) )

A var_ant outputs a string.  The predicate in the simple_criterion must be
one that is defined for the particular singular it follows and also one
that evaluates to a string value.

Example:

	$(FUNC base)	*** ERROR	'base' is undefined for FUNC
	$(FUNC ctor)	*** ERROR	'ctor' evaluates to a Boolean  
	$(FUNC type)	*** OK		prints the return type of the current FUNC


modifier_list:
	modifier_list(opt) modifier

modifier:
	mod_0
	mod_1 string

mod_0:
	cap

mod_1:
	trim:
	mod:

Modifiers change the output text.  They may be repeated
and operate sequentially.

cap		- capitalize text
trim:	- truncate text (argument is length)
mod:	- edit text (argument is sed-style edit command)

As of 1/6/92 the only sed special characters that mod:
recognizes are ^ (string string beginning) and $ (string end).
Redundant /'s may be left off: e.g. /foo// may be written foo/

Example:

Where CLASS is in file "file.hxx"

	$(CLASS file: mod: .hxx/ cap) 

prints
	
	FILE


** Assignments and Seperators **

assig_ant:
	$ ( SET word rval )

word:
	(any sequence of alphanumerics)

$(SET foo bar) assigns the value 'bar' to the variable 'foo'
(which does not need to be declared).  Foo can be any alpha-
numeric string which is not otherwise used as a variable or
a Formic keyword.  Bar can be any string or derived_string.
The variable retains its value until re-set.

Example:

$(SET flag noInt)
$(FOR FUNCS)
$	(IF FUNC type: int)
$		(SET flag hasInt)
$	(FI)
$(ROF)$///

$(IF flag name: hasInt)$///
	Integer
$(FI)

This script prints the words "Integer" if and only if
at least one function in its input returns an int.


sep_ant:
	$ ( SEP string )

$(SEP foo) evaluates to the string foo if the immediately
enclosing loop is not on its final iteration, or the null
string if it is.  Thus $(SEP ,) is a shorter way of
writing $(IF N more),$(FI)


** Plurals **

plural:
	CLASSES
	FUNCS
	FUNCS_UPWARDS
	VARS
	ATTRS
	ARGS
	SUPERCLASSES
	SUBCLASSES
	SIBCLASSES
	SUBFUNCS
	SIBFUNCS
	REFCLASSES


A plural is a list of objects of a particular type over which
a FOR loop can iterate.

				-type-	-sets-			-content-

CLASSES			CLASS	CLASS			all classes 

FUNCS			FUNC	FUNC 			member functions of current
										class, or global functions if none

FUNCS_UPWARDS	FUNC	FUNC 			member functions of current 
										class and all of its superclasses

VARS			VAR		VAR 			member variables of current class

ATTRS			ATTR	ATTR			class attributes of current class

ARGS			ARG		ARG				parameters of current function

SUPERCLASSES	CLASS	SUPERCLASS		superclasses of current class
										(not including self)

SUBCLASSES		CLASS	SUBCLASS 		subclasses of current class
										(not including self)

SIBCLASSES		CLASS	SIBCLASS 		sibling classes of current class
										i.e. all subclasses of its superclass 
										(self is included)

SUBFUNCS		FUNC	SUBFUNC 		sub-implementations of current function
										i.e all functions that override it

SIBFUNCS		FUNC	SIBFUNC 		sibling functions of current function
										i.e. all member function of the same class
										(self is included)
										
REFCLASSES		CLASS	REFCLASS		clases referred to by paramenters or
										the return type of the current function


** Singulars **

singular:
	word
	N
	FNAME
	CLASS
	FUNC
	VAR
	ATTR
	ARG
	SUPERCLASS
	SUBCLASS
	SIBCLASS
	SUBFUNC
	SIBFUNC
	REFCLASS

A singular is a variable with a particular type as a value.
Some are global and others are iteration variables of FOR loops.

			-type-	-set by-				-content-

word		STRING	$(SET word rval) or		(programmer defined)
					command line -D 

FNAME		STRING	X++ parser				file name (path info stripped)

N			COUNT	$(FOR <anything>)		iteration count

CLASS		CLASS	$(FOR CLASSES)			current class 

FUNC		FUNC	$(FOR FUNCS) or 		current member function
					$(FOR FUNCS_UPWARDS)	or global function if not in
											$(FOR CLASSES) loop

VAR			VAR		$(FOR VARS) 			current member variable

ATTR		ATTR	$(FOR ATTRS)			current class attribute

ARG			ARG		$(FOR ARGS)				current function parameter

SUPERCLASS	CLASS	$(FOR SUPERCLASSES) 	current superclass of current class

SUBCLASS	CLASS	$(FOR SUBCLASSES) 		current subclass of current class

SIBCLASS	CLASS	$(FOR SIBCLASSES) 		current sibling class of current class
											(self is included)

SUBFUNC		FUNC	$(FOR SUBFUNCES) 		current sub-implementation of current
											function where "sub-implementaion of
											member function F of class C" is defined
											as any member function of any subclass of
											C that overrides F

SIBFUNC		FUNC	$(FOR SIBFUNCES) 		current sibling function of current
											function (self is included)
										
REFCLASS	CLASS	$(FOR REFCLASSES)		class referred to by paramenters or
											the return type of the current function


** Predicates (general) **

predicate_0:
	more		name		base		memf
	memv		comment		file		infiles
	type		ctor		dtor		root
	primtype 	primroot	ptr			mname
	out			defl 		overclass	overmname
	homeclass 	onum

predicate_1:
	plus:		minus:		equals:		name:
	kind:		memf:		memv:		pro:
	base:		basepro:	superpro:	attr:
	baseattr:	superattr:	type:		root:
	primtype:	primroot:	rootkind:	overclass:
	ptr:		spec:		postspec:	defl:
	file:		onum:		arg:		mname:

predicate_2:
	pro:mf:
	pro:mv:
	attr:arg:
	baseattr:arg:
	arg:is:

predicate_3:
	attr:arg:is:
	baseattr:arg:is:
	superattr:arg:is:
	

  Each predicate is applicable to one or more types of Formic
variable. They may used in criteria with singulars or plurals
that have an appropriate type.  Some predicates evaluate to
booleans others to strings.  Boolean predicates are meaningful
only in $(IF) $(FOR) and $(CASE) ants.  String predicates may
may be used as boolean predicates, in which case a null string
value maps to FALSE an a non-null to TRUE, and also in VAR ants
where the string is output. In a simple criterion the predicate
'name' may be omitted; e.g. $(CLASS) == $(CLASS name)

NOTE: With the predicates 'name:', 'type:', 'root:', 'primtype:' and
'primroot:' the '%' character may be used in the argument string as a
wildcard. E.g.  $(VAR type: s%) is TRUE for 'short foo' and 'signed bar',
but FALSE for 'int baz'.

** Predicates for type STRING **

Boolean: 				- contents -

name: x					string name is x


** Predicates for type COUNT **

String: 				- contents -

plus: x					count plus x
minus: x				count minus x

Boolean:				- TRUE if -

more					current iteration is not the last e.g $(IF N more)
equals: x				count equals x


** Predicates for type CLASS **

String: 				- contents -

name					name of class
file					file containing class declaration (path info stripped)
comment					class comment
base					name of parent class
attr:arg: x y			y-th argument of class attribute x
baseattr:arg: x y		y-th argument of class attribute x in parent class 

Boolean:				- TRUE if -

infiles					class is in one of the files specified by -F on the command line
memf					class has member functions
memv					class has member variables
name: x					class name is x
file: x					name of file containing class declaration is x
base: x					name of parent class is x 
kind: x					class is named x on a subclass of a class named x
memf: x					class has member function x
memv: x					class has member variable x
pro:  x					class has protection group x (e.g "public")
basepro: x				parent class has protection group x
superpro: x				class or some superclass has a protection group x
attr: x					class has attribute x
baseattr: x				parent class has attribute x
superattr: x			class or some superclasss has attribute x
pro:mf: x y				class has protection group x containing member fucntion y
pro:mv: x y				class has protection group x containing member variable y
attr:arg:is: x y z		class has attribute x with argument z at postion y
baseattr:arg:is: x y z	parent class has attribute x with argument z at postion y



** Predicates for type FUNC **

String: 				- contents -

name					name of function
mname					mangled name of function
file					file containing function declaration
comment					function comment
type					return type of function
root					type with indirection removed
primtype				type with typedefs resolved
primroot				type with indirection removed and typedefs resolved [1]
homeclass				class of which function is a member
overclass				superclass contining function that FUNC directly overrides
overmname				mangled name of corresponding function in overlcass
onum					arbitrary, locally unique int if function is overloaded, else null 

Boolean:				- TRUE if -

infiles					function is in one of the files specified by -F on the command line
comment					function has a comment
ctor					function is a constructor
dtor					function is a destructor
type					function has a return type (i.e. is not 'void')
overclass				function overrides function in some superclass
ptr						return type is a pointer
enumtype				return type is an enum
name: x					function name is x
mname: x				mangled name is x
file: x					function is declared in file x
overclass: x			function directly overrides a function in class x
type: x					return type is x
pro: x					protection type of function is x (e.g "public")
root: x					return type with indirection removed is x
primtype: x				return type with typedefs resolved is x
primroot: x				return type with indirection removed and typedefs resolved is x
rootkind: x				return type refers to a class which is x or a subclass of x
ptr: x					return type is a pointer to x
spec: x					return type contains type specifier x
postspec: x				function has postfixed specifier x (e.g. postfix 'const')
onum: x					onum (see above) of function is x


[1] NOTE: 'primroot' removes ALL indirection, including that
          included in the typedefs; e.g. for:

			typedef char* foo;
			foo* bar();

		  primroot of foo is 'char'.  The purpose of the
		  predicate is to yield the true type of the object
		  ultimately referred to.


** Predicates for type VAR **

String: 				- contents -

name					name of member variable
file					file containing variable declaration
type					type of variable
root					type with indirection removed
primtype				type with typedefs resolved
primroot				type with indirection removed and typedefs resolved

Boolean:				- TRUE if -

ptr						variable  type is a pointer
enumtype				variable type is an enum
name: x					variable name is x
type: x					variable type is x
pro: x					protection type of variable is x (e.g "public")
root: x					type with indirection removed is x
primtype: x				type with typedefs resolved is x
primroot: x				type with indirection removed and typedefs resolved is x
kind: x					type is a class which is x or a subclass of x
rootkind: x				type with indirection removed is class x or a subclass of x
ptr: x					type is a pointer to x
spec: x					type contains type specifier x


** Predicates for type ATTR **

String: 				- contents -

name					name of class attribute
